{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:0329ce6f5d6f4a1ccdd68c5b330cf9d617cc4b30a68130cf1949b6783f797d51","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.36},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"CONJUNCT A (OECD-38 GDP-weighted core CPI/PCE >= 2.50%/yr avg 2027-2036). I carry BOTH weight layers explicitly, because the question is a threshold question on a quantity whose uncertainty is dominated by weights, not by inflation mechanism.\nLayer 1, within-country basket (US-like core): shelter 0.33 @ 3.2 = 1.056; medical + social care services 0.09 @ 3.3 = 0.297; other labour-intensive core services 0.30 @ 2.7 = 0.810; core goods 0.23 @ 0.4 = 0.092; communication/digital 0.05 @ -0.8 = -0.040. Sum 2.215, plus electricity entering ONLY as second-round business cost (~2% energy share of service gross output x ~0.5 pass-through) +0.12 = 2.335. ACCOUNTING LOCK: electricity is excluded from US core CPI/PCE and from HICP core by construction, so a 4-6% power/construction/care skew against 0 to -1% software/media/tradables yields ~2.3% core, not ~3%. The skew is violent; the level is not.\nLayer 2, GDP weights across OECD-38 (2025 PPP shares): US 0.38 x 2.35 = 0.893; Japan 0.07 x 1.6 = 0.112; Germany 0.055 x 2.0 = 0.110; UK 0.043 x 2.5 = 0.108; France 0.040 x 1.9 = 0.076; Italy 0.033 x 2.0 = 0.066; Canada 0.025 x 2.2 = 0.055; Korea 0.035 x 2.0 = 0.070; Spain 0.027 x 2.2 = 0.059; Mexico 0.034 x 3.8 = 0.129; Turkey 0.035 x 10.0 = 0.350; Poland 0.020 x 3.2 = 0.064; Australia 0.020 x 2.5 = 0.050; remainder 0.18 x 2.3 = 0.414. SUM = 2.556%.\nTHE SWING TERM IS TURKEY, AND IT IS ARITHMETIC, NOT MACRO. Drop Turkey and renormalize: (2.556 - 0.350)/0.965 = 2.286%. So one member at a 3.5% PPP weight contributes +0.27pp to the aggregate and is the difference between clearing and missing the threshold. Sensitivity: Turkey at 6% avg -> aggregate 2.42 (FAILS); at 10% -> 2.56 (clears); at 14% -> 2.70. Path check: 2027-2030 at ~2.85 and 2031-2036 at ~2.37 averages (4 x 2.85 + 6 x 2.37)/10 = 2.56, matching the weighted build, so the hurdle is cleared by the front half plus the EM-OECD floor, not by 2030s stagflation. With sd 0.35 on a 10-year average and central 2.56: P = 1 - Phi((2.50-2.56)/0.35) = 0.57; haircut to 0.52 for resolver latitude that runs one-way DOWN (core-PCE-only reading -0.25pp on the US leg; consumption-PPP rather than GDP weighting lowers the EM contribution). P(A) = 0.52.\nCONJUNCT B (China headline CPI < 1.50%/yr avg 2027-2036), treated as a semi-independent object with its own basket. Food 0.19 @ 1.5 (pork cycles mean-revert, not trend) = 0.285; residence 0.21 @ 0.3 (rent-imputed, so a -55/-60% property adjustment barely registers but delivers no rent growth) = 0.063; other goods 0.25 @ -0.2 (capacity +5-7%/yr vs domestic demand +3-4%/yr, PPI leakage) = -0.050; services ex-residence 0.35 @ 1.6 (late-window working-age -1%/yr wage pressure, held down by a household income share stuck ~44% of GDP and pension subsidies already 10.1% of fiscal expenditure that cannot buy down precautionary saving) = 0.560. SUM = 0.858, call central 1.05 after allowing for the 2026 basket rebase raising the services weight. Distance to threshold 0.45pp; sd on a 10-yr average 0.60pp (tails: deliberate reflation, RMB depreciation import pass-through, one +5% food year adds 0.5pp to the decade average). P = Phi(0.45/0.60) = Phi(0.75) = 0.77 -> 0.75.\nCORRELATION AND MULTIPLICATION. Mixed sign, netting slightly negative: Chinese goods deflation is itself the force holding my OECD core-goods term at 0.4%, so conditioning on A buys little extra on B through the tradables channel (negative), while the common \"OECD physical/care scarcity vs China demand deficit\" bifurcation is positive, and tariff/fragmentation walls partly sever the negative channel. rho = -0.10 -> P(B|A) = 0.75 x 0.93 = 0.698. JOINT = 0.52 x 0.698 = 0.363 -> 36.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.617Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Turkish annual CPI, as published by TurkStat, averages below 7.0% over calendar 2027-2029 (three-year arithmetic mean of the December-on-December prints), with Mexico, Chile and Colombia each averaging below 3.5% over the same three years.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the EM-OECD floor that my CQ2 depends on: the GDP-weighted OECD-38 core aggregate falls from 2.556 to ~2.40, and P(core >= 2.50 over 2027-2036) drops from 0.52 to ~0.33. CQ2 moves from 36 to ~24. It also lowers the back-half core central from 2.30 to ~2.15, cutting CQ6-C from 15 to ~10 and raising D from 17 to ~22, with B roughly unchanged. This is the cleanest single falsifier of my numbers because it attacks the term I have made the largest and most mechanical.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US CPI shelter (BLS series for shelter, December-on-December) averages below 2.4%/yr over calendar 2027-2030, while US medical care services plus the social-assistance component of PCE services averages below 2.6%/yr over the same four years.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Breaks the two line items that carry 1.35pp of my 2.335 US core build. US core falls to ~1.95, the OECD-38 aggregate to ~2.40, and the front-loaded path I rely on (2.85 then 2.37) collapses to a flat ~2.2. CQ2 moves from 36 to ~22; CQ6-C from 15 to ~9 and D from 17 to ~24. It would also confirm the state-monopsony-rations-care channel over the care-prices-clear channel, which lowers CQ1's second conjunct further (from 0.12 to ~0.07) and so CQ1 from 8 to ~5.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Announced hyperscaler plus utility-scale firm-capacity additions serving data centres in the OECD-38 and China reach cumulative in-service (commercial-operation-date) capacity of at least 70 GW of net firm supply by end-2031, as reported in EIA/ENTSO-E/CEC interconnection and commissioning data, with cumulative publicly disclosed AI-related capex impairments and cancellations below 10% of announced buildouts.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my coupled claim that the physical trades and interconnection bind harder than the dollars. AI-Q4 resolves toward NO, so my 2032-2036 window is NOT the post-bust half: capital-services growth stays at ~1.8%/yr (+0.30pp on the OECD leg), the AI savings-demand term returns to +0.25pp on r*. CQ3 P50 moves from 1.65 to ~1.95 and P90 to ~2.9; CQ4 P50 from 1.95 to ~2.30; CQ6-A from 10 to ~17, D from 17 to ~11, B to ~57. CQ5's first conjunct falls (back-half MOVE nearer 85) taking CQ5 from 25 to ~20.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's NBS headline CPI prints a calendar-year average of 2.0% or higher in any two consecutive years during 2027-2031, with the non-food, non-residence component contributing at least 1.0pp of it (i.e. not a pork or energy spike).\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills CQ2's stronger conjunct. Two years at 2.0%+ raise the 10-year average by ~0.2pp from my 1.05 central and, more importantly, signal that the household-income-share and property channels have reflated; P(China CPI < 1.50%) falls from 0.75 to ~0.40 and CQ2 from 36 to ~20. It would also raise China's nominal demand path, lifting my China real leg toward 3.3% and the combined CQ3 P50 to ~1.80, and shifting ~4pp of CQ6 mass from D into B and C.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD harmonized unemployment (OECD-total, Short-Term Labour Market Statistics) averages 5.3% or below across calendar 2029-2031 while Eurostat SES 2030 and US BLS OES show ISIC F+Q median gross hourly earnings outpacing ISIC J+K+M by at least 4.5 percentage points cumulatively from 2022/2025 to 2030.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the only signpost that can rescue CQ1 and it attacks my composition arithmetic directly: a realized 4.5pp gap by 2030 implies the differential is running at ~0.8-0.9pp/yr rather than my +0.02pp/yr net, meaning either the entrant-dilution deduction (-0.40) or the J+K+M survivor-median deduction (-0.40) is wrong. Conjunct B's marginal rises from 0.12 to ~0.45 and conjunct A to ~0.60, taking CQ1 from 8 to ~32. It would also imply the care bottleneck is clearing through PRICES, which raises my back-half core central by ~0.2pp and moves CQ6-C from 15 to ~21.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 2 fermi seat, effort 'inflation'."}}